commit c3b2653621e52cfea2662e8762b9d5dfa46b1620
Author: Glen Taggart <glenmtaggart@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun May 31 20:00:21 2026 -0700

    Resolve typevar defaults/values faithfully; add TypeVarValueOption
    
    Make typevar resolution agree with what subscription/type-checkers see, and
    give TypeVarValue an honest static surface.
    
    Default & value coercion (_build_subs):
    - Coerce a typevar default the way subscription normalizes an explicit arg
      (None -> NoneType, "Fwd" -> ForwardRef), so `C` and `C[default]` agree.
    - Resolve positional args and defaults that *reference another typevar* against
      the bindings so far -- `class C[T, U = T]` with `C[int]` now yields `int` for
      `U`, not the raw TypeVar (PEP 696 auto-fills the default into __args__
      unsubstituted, so this lives in the positional branch).
    - Same treatment for the bound fallback.
    
    _substitute_typevars now handles unions and typing special forms, which it
    previously couldn't reconstruct:
    - Unions (X | Y / typing.Union): rebuild via `|`, collapsing identical members;
      a union with an unresolved (NoDefault) member is itself unresolved.
    - Callable: explicit `([params], ret)` rebuild covering both the typing and
      collections.abc spellings.
    - Annotated/ClassVar/Final/Literal: rebuild via copy_with over flat __args__.
    - Any other parameterized form (e.g. a PEP 695 generic `type` alias) is refused
      with a clear TypeError instead of crashing or silently corrupting the type.
    
    TypeVarValue / TypeVarValueOption:
    - TypeVarValue is now honestly typed `type[T]`: reading it where the typevar is
      unbound with no default (or only partially resolved) raises LookupError rather
      than leaking the NoDefault sentinel. The unresolved check is recursive, so
      holes nested in list[NoDefault] / Callable[[T], ...] are caught too.
    - New TypeVarValueOption, typed `type[T] | None`, yields None in that case for
      the canonical optional idiom. The default-coercion guarantees the None is
      unambiguous (a None default resolves to the truthy NoneType, never None).
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
